Q5. What is 'marker interface' in java ?
    A marker interface is a interface that does not declare any method.
    basically empty interface, it is also known as tag interface
    ex:
        Serializable, Cloneable, Remote interface
    Why Do We Need It?
    Marker interfaces are used to give metadata or signals to the Java runtime or tools that a class has a special behavior or permission.

    Real-Life Analogy
    Imagine a movie set. Actors with a VIP badge get access to special areas. The badge doesn’t say anything — it just shows you’re VIP.
    Similarly, a marker interface is like a VIP badge for Java classes — it doesn't do anything itself, but the system checks for it to make decisions.

a. Serializable
Serializable is a marker interface in java.io package — it marks a class as serializable, meaning its objects can be converted to a byte stream (to save to a file, send over a network, etc.).


b. Cloneable
JDK uses this interface in such a way that, CloneNotSupportedException is thrown if item to be cloned does not implement cloneable interface.

Uses of marker interface
marker interface indicates some special characteristics or features associated with the implementing class.
we deign custom marker interface in java for specific application requirement.

Q6. How to make class immutable in Java ?
ans: Immutable class in java are those classes who's instances cannot be modified
     example of immutable classes in java are "String" and all wrapper classes.

    To make class immutable
    . Make the class final
    . mark all the fields private and final
    . remove setter methods
    . make constructor initialize all fields
    . avoid returning mutable objects

    After java 16, java introduced "record" which make class immutable.

Q7: What is record and how it works ?
ans: A record in Java is a special type of class introduced in Java 14 (preview) and officially added in Java 16.
     It is used to store immutable data — like a data carrier or DTO (Data Transfer Object) — without writing a lot of boilerplate code

     or
     A record is a compact way to create an immutable class with built-in equals(), hashCode(), and toString() methods.

     Example:
     public record Employee(String name, int age) { }

     That’s it! 😄
     Java automatically creates:
     private final fields (name, age)
     Constructor
     Getters (name(), age())
     equals(), hashCode(), and toString()


