What is Default and Static Methods in Interfaces.?
  Default Methods (Java 8+)
    What they are: Methods in an interface with a body (implementation) marked with the default keyword.
    Why they exist: To add new methods to existing interfaces without forcing all old classes to update, ensuring backward compatibility.
    How they work: Implementing classes can use the provided default code, or they can override it with their own specific logic.
    Example: A List interface adding a forEach(Consumer action) default method. Old ArrayLists automatically get it, but you can still override it if needed.

   example:
    interface MyInterface {
        default void greet() { // Default implementation
            System.out.println("Hello from default method!");
        }
    }

    class MyClass implements MyInterface {
        // Can use the default greet() or override it:
        // @Override
        // public void greet() { System.out.println("Hello from MyClass!"); }
    }

  Static Methods in Interfaces (Java 8+)
    What they are: Utility methods associated with the interface itself, not any object.
    Why they exist: For helper or factory methods related to the interface, like Collections.sort() (though usually in utility classes).
    How they work: Called directly on the interface name (e.g., MyInterface.doSomething()), cannot be overridden.
    Example: An Optional interface might have Optional.of(value) as a static factory method.

    example:
       interface MathUtils {
           static int add(int a, int b) { // Static utility method
               return a + b;
           }
       }

       // Call:
       // int sum = MathUtils.add(5, 3); // Works, no object needed

  Key Differences
  Calling: Default methods use an "object" (myObj.greet()); static methods use the interface name (MathUtils.add()).
  Inheritance: Default methods can be overridden; static methods cannot.
  Purpose: Default adds features to interfaces; static provides interface-level utilities.